WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.266
Hello! Welcome to our last
section in the National Data tab.

2
00:00:06.266 --> 00:00:09.395
So far, we've explored
data on demographics,

3
00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:12.666
community living, and
community participation.

4
00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:16.790
In this video, we will explore
data on work and economics.

5
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.620
Let's start by clicking on the National Data
tab on the top left corner of the page.

6
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.389
Under Category, select Work and Economic.

7
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.773
In this video, we will
explore poverty rates,

8
00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:34.274
employment rates, median
earnings, and household burden.

9
00:00:34.274 --> 00:00:37.713
We will start by exploring
data on those who live below

10
00:00:37.713 --> 00:00:41.089
the poverty line and are
between the ages of 18 to 64.

11
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:47.499
The U.S. Census defines that an individual
who makes less than $16,000 a year

12
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.903
or a couple who makes
less than $21,000 a year,

13
00:00:50.903 --> 00:00:56.303
or a family who makes less than $32,000
a year live below the poverty line.

14
00:00:56.303 --> 00:00:58.750
In this section, we have two maps.

15
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.422
The one on the left shows
people with disabilities,

16
00:01:02.422 --> 00:01:06.669
and the one on the right shows
people without disabilities.

17
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.450
Under each map, it gives you a
key on what these colors mean.

18
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.373
And as we're familiar
from previous videos,

19
00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:16.190
the darker the color
means more opportunity.

20
00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.829
The lighter colors mean more disparity.

21
00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:21.918
So in this case, lighter
colors indicate a higher

22
00:01:21.918 --> 00:01:25.260
percentage of people who
live below the poverty line.

23
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.214
The map of people with
disabilities is almost

24
00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:31.861
entirely made up of these light colors,

25
00:01:31.861 --> 00:01:34.915
meaning they have higher
rates of people with

26
00:01:34.915 --> 00:01:37.969
disabilities who live
below the poverty line.

27
00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:44.210
Whereas the map of people without disabilities
is made up of these darker colors.

28
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.584
Generally, these maps show
that a higher percentage of

29
00:01:47.584 --> 00:01:50.690
people with disabilities
live below the poverty line.

30
00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:55.800
Let's scroll down to the data table
and look at specific information.

31
00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:01.068
To find the state with the
highest level of poverty,

32
00:02:01.068 --> 00:02:04.980
click on the top of the banner,
and then click it a second time.

33
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.990
I see that in Puerto Rico.

34
00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:12.155
Nearly 49% of people with
disabilities between

35
00:02:12.155 --> 00:02:15.635
the ages of 18 to 64 live
below the poverty line.

36
00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:20.747
That's almost half of the entire
population of people with disabilities.

37
00:02:20.747 --> 00:02:22.309
That's a large amount.

38
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.747
Comparatively, about
34.9% of people without

39
00:02:25.747 --> 00:02:29.621
disabilities in this age
live below the poverty line.

40
00:02:29.621 --> 00:02:35.616
That's still a huge amount, however, nearly
twice as many people with disabilities

41
00:02:35.616 --> 00:02:38.759
live below the poverty
line in Puerto Rico.

42
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:43.585
Please explore the other poverty age
group categories in your own time.

43
00:02:43.585 --> 00:02:47.300
Let's scroll back up to the top
and focus on employment data.

44
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:55.189
Under topic, you'll scroll under
employment, Click on Unemployed Age 18-64.

45
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.710
This shows us two maps.

46
00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.450
Right now, we're currently
looking at the amount of people

47
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:08.212
with disabilities and without
disabilities who are unemployed.

48
00:03:08.212 --> 00:03:12.035
Unemployed is defined as people
who are not currently employed,

49
00:03:12.035 --> 00:03:14.159
but are actively looking for a job.

50
00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.051
Just like in this last section,

51
00:03:17.051 --> 00:03:22.031
unemployment is considered a negative
outcome, so we want to see less of it.

52
00:03:22.031 --> 00:03:23.931
We want to see darker colors,

53
00:03:23.931 --> 00:03:27.600
which represent a lower
percentage of unemployed people.

54
00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:30.154
To change the color scheme at any point,

55
00:03:30.154 --> 00:03:33.510
click on the display button on the
bottom right corner of the page.

56
00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.450
Let's look at the map.

57
00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:40.259
Remember, you can drag your mouse over any
of the states to see the exact percentage

58
00:03:40.259 --> 00:03:42.435
of people with disabilities or without

59
00:03:42.435 --> 00:03:45.069
disabilities who are
unemployed in that state.

60
00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:49.873
On the map of people with disabilities,
there are more lighter colors,

61
00:03:49.873 --> 00:03:53.111
so more people with
disabilities who are unemployed

62
00:03:53.111 --> 00:03:55.650
compared to people without disabilities.

63
00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.219
You may pause and reflect why this is.

64
00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:03.659
You can also explore if this
is true in your state as well.

65
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.823
Let's look at Texas, for example.

66
00:04:06.823 --> 00:04:11.750
So I'm going to scroll down to find
specific information on Texas.

67
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:20.490
About 5.3% of people with disabilities
between the ages of 18 to 64 are unemployed.

68
00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.023
That number may not sound like a lot,

69
00:04:24.023 --> 00:04:28.169
however, that represents
nearly 100,000 Texans.

70
00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:34.519
Comparatively, about 3.9% of Texans
without disabilities are unemployed.

71
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.649
So in Texas, people with disabilities
have a higher unemployment rate.

72
00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.067
There are two more employment categories.

73
00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:45.889
I recommend exploring these and finding
data related to your own state.

74
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.980
Let's go under topic and
explore median earnings.

75
00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:57.223
The median earnings is different
than the average earnings.

76
00:04:57.223 --> 00:05:00.866
The median tells us what
the number is halfway

77
00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:04.192
between the smallest and largest earnings.

78
00:05:04.192 --> 00:05:10.449
Another way to think about it is that 50%
of people earn less than this number.

79
00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.159
And 50% of people earn
more than this number.

80
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:20.282
These maps are a little bit different
than what we've seen before.

81
00:05:20.282 --> 00:05:22.174
Instead of percentages,

82
00:05:22.174 --> 00:05:27.524
the map shows us the median earning
in the state in U.S. dollars.

83
00:05:27.524 --> 00:05:33.449
So, the lighter colors represent a
median earning of $18,000 to $31,000.

84
00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.390
In this map, these are on the lower range.

85
00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:42.059
The darkest colors represent a median
earning of $45,000 to $84,000,

86
00:05:42.059 --> 00:05:47.087
which are the highest earnings.
So, we want to see darker colors.

87
00:05:47.087 --> 00:05:52.810
Generally, looking at these two maps,
we can notice a distinct difference.

88
00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:56.434
People without disabilities
often have higher

89
00:05:56.434 --> 00:05:59.919
median income than people
with disabilities.

90
00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:05.659
Browse through the data table below and
compare the median income in your state.

91
00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:09.300
Let's scroll back to the top
and explore household burden.

92
00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.698
There are two types of household burden.

93
00:06:13.698 --> 00:06:17.709
There is burden experienced by those
who own a home and have a mortgage,

94
00:06:17.709 --> 00:06:19.380
and those who rent their home.

95
00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.629
Let's take a look at housing burden
for those who rent their home.

96
00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:28.400
A household is considered to be
rent burden when a renter spends

97
00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.524
more than 30% of their income
on house… on housing costs.

98
00:06:32.524 --> 00:06:35.779
So this includes rent
and utilities combined.

99
00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.239
Let's look at the map.

100
00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:40.620
Instead of two maps, there's just one.

101
00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:45.443
It shows the percent of households
that are considered rent burdened.

102
00:06:45.443 --> 00:06:49.170
This includes both people with
and without disabilities.

103
00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:53.980
The key below the map tells us
exactly what these colors means.

104
00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.339
Renting burden is considered a negative
outcome, so we want to see less of it.

105
00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.357
So we want to see states
with darker colors.

106
00:07:03.357 --> 00:07:07.340
The lighter colors mean that more
people are burdened by rent.

107
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.060
To find more specific information, I
want to scroll down to the data table.

108
00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.279
When I click on the arrow.

109
00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:18.933
I want to find the state or territory
with the most housing burden,

110
00:07:18.933 --> 00:07:23.078
so I'm going to click, and then
I'm going to click a second time.

111
00:07:23.078 --> 00:07:26.203
I can see that Florida has
the highest percentage

112
00:07:26.203 --> 00:07:31.560
of households that are considered rent
burden, which is about 56% of the population.

113
00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.885
This means that more
than half of the people

114
00:07:34.885 --> 00:07:39.530
who live in Florida are burdened
by rent. That's a lot of people.

115
00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:46.570
Alright, so that concludes our section on
work economics in the National Data tab.

116
00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:51.847
We explored data on poverty, employment,
earnings, and household burden.

117
00:07:51.847 --> 00:07:54.779
Stay tuned for information on city data.
